Chiropractic marketing that fills a schedule has three parts, not one: ads that reach people in pain near you, a system that answers and books every enquiry within minutes, and finished patients who refer. Most practices buy the first part, skip the second, and wait for the third. The schedule stays flat because the three only work together.

This guide is the plan we run for chiropractors, in the order we switch it on, with one real clinic as the example. We have included the slow months, because that is where most owners give up.

## The three sources of new chiropractic patients

Ask a chiropractor where new patients come from and most will say "referrals, mostly". That is true for a practice that has been open ten years, and it is why growth stalls: referrals only grow as fast as the number of finished patients, and that number is set by everything else.

- **Ads.** Meta and Google ads shown to people within a realistic drive of the practice. This is the only source you can turn up on purpose.
- **A system that answers.** Someone, or something, that replies to every call, form and message in minutes, books the visit, reminds the patient and follows up when they go quiet. Without it, the ads pay for enquiries nobody answers.
- **Referrals and walk-ins.** Finished patients who send a friend, leave a review, or mention you at work. This source is free, and it is the last one to arrive.

Our [chiropractor marketing page](/industries/chiropractic/) describes how we set up each stage. What follows is what it looked like at one clinic.

## What it looked like at Central Clinic, month by month

Central Clinic of Chiropractic is a two-chiropractor practice in downtown Sumter, South Carolina. It offers Gonstead care and decompression and is private pay, so every new patient has to be found and persuaded, not sent over by an insurer. We have run its marketing since January 2026, and the full story is on our [Central Clinic case study](/results/central-clinic-of-chiropractic/). The figures below are from that page.

**January 2026: everything switched on together.** Meta and Google ads, around $3k a month, shown to people in and around Sumter. Every click lands on a page built to book. An assistant answers messages on social media and the website at any hour, answers questions, and books the consultation itself. Automatic follow-ups run until the visit is booked, reminders go out before it, and a receptionist line covers the phone when staff are busy.

**Months one to three: hard going.** Patients came in one at a time. The ads were running, the system was answering, and the schedule still looked thin. This is the point where most owners stop, decide the ads do not work, and go back to referrals. Dr. Jordan kept going.

**Month four onward: referrals and walk-ins on top.** The first patients had finished care. Some sent friends. Review requests after care meant happy patients said so on Google, which the next person searching could see. Foot traffic grew, and the ads kept delivering at the same budget.

**Today: 30+ qualified new patients a month, on average.** Still a client, and the system still runs the whole journey, from the first message to the win-back email months later. In Dr. Jordan Reizer's words:

> DocsScale runs our ads, our messages and our follow-ups. We just see the patients. Easy to work with, and they get it done.

The point of the timeline is not the number at the end. It is that the number was built in months one to three, when it was hardest to believe in.

## Why the first three months are slow

People ask us whether the slow start could have been skipped. We do not think so, for three reasons that apply to any chiropractic practice.

Ads take a few weeks to learn who responds, and a new clinic account has no history to learn from. Every change to the audience or the offer starts part of that over again.

Referrals cannot exist before finished patients do. If a course of care takes six to twelve weeks, the first referral is a quarter away on the day you switch the ads on, however good they are.

Reviews build the same way. The person searching for a chiropractor in month one sees the reviews you had before you started. The ones from the new patients arrive after their care, not before.

So the first quarter is paid for by ads alone, and it looks expensive. From month four the free sources start paying in, and the same ad budget looks very different.

## The order to switch things on

If you are starting from referrals only, this is the order we would do it in. Each step makes the next one cheaper.

1. **Answer first.** Before any ad, make sure every call, form and message gets a reply within minutes, including at 8 pm. Send your own practice a message tonight and time the answer. If it is tomorrow morning, fix this before you spend.
1. **Book and remind.** Give every enquiry one tap to a time, and send a confirmation and a reminder before the visit. First-visit no-shows are where ad money quietly disappears.
1. **Contact the patients you already have.** People who finished care a year ago and were never invited back are the cheapest appointments you will ever get. Our [patient reactivation](/services/patient-reactivation/) page explains how we run that.
1. **Then the ads.** One clear new-patient offer, one local audience, one page built to book. Our [paid advertising](/services/paid-ads/) page covers how we build them.
1. **Ask for the review, every time.** After care, not before, and in a way that makes it one tap.

Do the first three and your ads start from a practice that converts. Do only the fourth and you are paying to send people in pain to a voicemail.

## What to measure

Clicks and "leads" tell you almost nothing. Ask for four numbers every week, from whoever runs your marketing or from your own software:

- Enquiries that got a reply within five minutes, as a share of all enquiries
- First visits booked
- First visits that showed up
- New Google reviews

If those four are moving, the schedule follows. If an agency cannot show you them, that is the thing to fix before the budget goes up.

## Before you decide it is not working

Give the whole system a year, and judge it on booked and kept visits. Look at the three sources separately: if the ads are producing enquiries and the schedule is still thin, the problem is the answer and the booking, not the ads. If enquiries are answered and booked and still nobody refers, the problem is the care experience or the review request, and no ad budget fixes that.

Central Clinic's first quarter looked like a reason to stop. Its second year looks like the reason it did not.

